{{- /*
  blog/update-post-in-bead — write a revised post body back into the bead's
  description. The blog suite treats the bead description as the single source
  of truth for the current post body, so any prompt that rewrites the post
  (e.g. polish) persists the new body here via `bd update --body-file`.

  Assumes the caller already ran blog/shared/load-post-from-bead, so
  `$post_file` holds the PREVIOUS body (used to produce a diff for the audit
  trail).

  Callers pass named fields via a `dict`:

      Target: the bead ID whose description to overwrite. Required.

  Usage:

      {{ template "blog/shared/update-post-in-bead" (dict "Target" $target) }}
*/ -}}
{{- $id := .Target -}}
Persist the revised post body back into the bead's description. Write the new
body to a temp file, show a diff against the previous body for the audit trail,
then overwrite the description with `bd update --body-file` (do NOT paste large
bodies inline — shell quoting on multi-line Markdown is fragile):

```bash
new_file=$(mktemp "/tmp/blog-{{ $id }}-new-XXXXXX.md")
cat > "$new_file" <<'MITTO_POST'
<the full revised post body>
MITTO_POST
# Audit trail: the description IS the post, so diff old vs new here (there is
# no git working tree for the body during the drafting phase).
diff -u "$post_file" "$new_file" || true
bd update {{ $id }} --body-file "$new_file"
rm -f "$new_file"
echo "updated description of {{ $id }}"
```
